  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nMostly clear skies continue overhead, and with fairly calm winds,\ntemperatures are on the chilly side this morning. At 2 AM, most \ninland valleys were in the 40s, with a few spots in the upper \n30s, and mountain valleys in the 20s. Today will be fair but cool\nafter the chilly start, with highs in the mid 60s to lower 70s for\nlower elevations. That's about 4-10 degrees below average for \nvalleys, and about 1-3 degrees below average at the coast. The \nnext storm Thursday will be pretty strong, with a well-defined \nfront moving through SoCal. Rain should begin in the L.A. Basin \nThursday morning, moving southeast into Riverside and San Diego \nCounties through the afternoon. Rain will be pretty intense along \nthe front, and there is a small chance of thunderstorms. For both \nreasons, rainfall rates could reach 0.50-0.65 per hour, which \ncould produce some urban flooding. Snow levels will start out \nhigh, and drop to about 6,500 feet by the time the precipitation \nmoves out. A few inches should accumulate above 7,500 feet, on the\nupper ski slopes around Big Bear. Down at Big Bear Lake, little \nif any accumulations are expected. But the timing of the cold air \nmoving in with the moisture are uncertain, so there's a range of \npotential snowfall outcomes. Winds will be strongest ahead of and \nalong the front, with gusts exceeding 25-30 mph for the coast and \n40-55 mph in the mountains and adjacent desert slopes, where \nthere's a wind advisory. Showers will follow the front Thursday \nevening as the snow level lowers, but then will eventually end \nThursday night. Friday will start out quite cold, but clouds will \nclear to bring mostly sunny conditions. A secondary shortwave \nrotates through the Great Basin Friday, but for now it doesn't \nappear to prolong the precipitation, just postpone any warm up. A \ntouch of offshore flow pays a visit Friday night and Saturday, but\nany brisk offshore winds will be quite isolated in foothills. \nHigh pressure aloft doesn't build much, but enough to provide dry \nand slightly warmer weather Friday through the weekend. \nTemperatures will continue below normal each of those days, with \nhighs in the mid 60s to lower 70s at lower elevations, upper 30s \nto mid 50s in the mountains, and 50s in the High Desert. Model \nguidance is starting to narrow the possibilities regarding the \ntiming of the next precipitation. A weak shortwave moves into the \nregion Sunday, bringing light showers Sunday night and Monday. \nRight now this system looks much weaker regarding precipitation \namounts and winds. By Tuesday there's some guidance showing a \ncontinuation of the unsettled, occasionally wet and windy pattern.\nBut there are nearly equal numbers of ensemble members showing a \nstronger ridge pattern for dry and warmer weather heading into the\nholiday.",
